          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em scheme
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            that
            %em locally
            looks like a particularly simple
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+space"} ringed space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+scheme"} affine scheme
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+ringed+spaces"} locally ringed spaces
            or within the category of presheaves of sets on the category of affine schemes
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Aff
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aff
          %p
            The notion of scheme originated in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            where it is, since
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
          %p However, the idea that
          .standout
            %p A scheme is a ringed space that is locally isomorphic to an affine space.
          %p
            is much more general and need not be restricted to the locality in Zariski topology and to a notion of affine spaces that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} formal duals
            of rings. If one takes another subcanonical Grothendieck topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Aff
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aff
            then one talks about
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi τ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
                \-locally affine spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Loc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Loc
            replacing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Aff
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aff
            and suitable topology and consider sheaves on it, as locally affine space in this generalized sense. The category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Loc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Loc
            can sometimes be represented by ringed spaces of special type and the gluing can be sometimes made in a genuine (classical, not Grothendieck) topology, within the category of ringed spaces.
          %p
            For instance a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            is a ringed space locally isomorphic to a “smooth affine space”
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
          %p
            The standard concept of scheme in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            is therefore usefully understood as a special case of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+scheme"} generalized scheme
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            and many other topics.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p Throughout this article, “ring” will mean “commutative ring with unit”.
          %h3#as_locally_ringed_spaces As locally ringed spaces
          %p
            A
            %strong scheme
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+ringed+space"} locally ringed space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \mathcal{O}_X)
            such that, for every point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U
            such that the locally ringed space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U, \mathcal{O}_{X} | U)
            is isomorphic to an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+scheme"} affine scheme
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+spectrum"} ring spectrum
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Spec
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mrow
                        %mi Spec
                        %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec A = (|Spec A|, \mathcal{O}_{Spec A})
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{X} | U
            denotes the restriction of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{X}
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^{*}(\mathcal{O}_{X})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: U \hookrightarrow X
            is the inclusion map, and
