                    %mrow
                      %mi Aff
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aff
          %p
            The standard reference for the functor-of-points approach to schemes is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#DG"} Demazure-Gabriel
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              Different authors take different approaches to the underlying set-theoretic issues. The astute reader will have noticed that we consider the category of
              %em all
              functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CRing
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CRing \to Set
              – which is not a locally small category. Nor is the category of sheaves on the site
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi CRing
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CRing^{op}
              with its Zariski topology actually a Grothendieck topos. With due regard to such set-theoretic issues, this approach seems to be conceptually the simplest. Or, one could keep one’s options open: for some suitable small category of rings left to one’s discretion, for example the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+presented+object"} finitely presented
              rings, one can consider schemes locally modelled on that category.
            %p
              Demazure-Gabriel steer a middle course involving
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universes"} universes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \in U \in V
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \to Set
              from small rings (called “models”) to (not necessarily small) sets. They remark that the device of using universes is really just a convenience that could mostly be dispensed with: one could work within the standard Bernays-Gödel framework by assuming that the models are inclusive enough to hold various standard commutative algebra constructions (e.g., quotients, localizations, completions) while still remaining a small category. However, since they wish to avail themselves of direct limits in the category of models, they choose to work with universes instead.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes+are+sober"} schemes are sober
          %p
            The category of schemes admits
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+coproducts"} small coproducts
          %p
            It does not admit
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizers"} coequalizers
            %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/questions/9961/colimits-of-schemes/23966#23966"} https://mathoverflow.net/questions/9961/colimits-of-schemes/23966#23966
          %p
            The category of schemes admits
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
          %p
            It does not admit
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+products"} infinite products
            %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/questions/9134/arbitrary-products-of-schemes-dont-exist-do-they/65534#65534"} https://mathoverflow.net/questions/9134/arbitrary-products-of-schemes-dont-exist-do-they/65534#65534
          %p (…)
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            this is a basic object of study, since the revolution of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+schemes"} relative schemes
            (which are just objects in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
            = succeed ")," do
